---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/intermediate/earthquakes-and-seismology">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Earth Science lesson. It builds on the foundational
    <em>Journey to the Centre of the Earth</em> — the layered interior whose discovery
    story reappears here as seismology's greatest result — and on <em>The Rock Cycle</em>
    for how rock responds to stress. No physics beyond springs and waves is assumed;
    everything else is developed as it is needed.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain the elastic-rebound mechanism: strain accumulates while the fault is locked, then snaps;</li>
    <li>name the three fault families and the stress direction each records;</li>
    <li>rank P-, S- and surface waves by speed and damage, and say what the P-minus-S lag is for;</li>
    <li>distinguish magnitude (one number, energy) from intensity (a map of shaking);</li>
    <li>state the honest line between earthquake forecasting (probabilities) and prediction (still unsolved).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Earthquakes are <strong>sudden releases of slowly stored plate strain</strong> — and the
    waves they generate are simultaneously the hazard and the instrument: the same signals that
    shake buildings let seismologists see inside the planet and, increasingly, warn before the
    shaking arrives. About a million earthquakes a year are detected worldwide, most too small
    to feel; the few dozen large ones each year account for some of the deadliest natural
    disasters on record. Understanding them means understanding three things in order: how rock
    stores and releases energy (the fault), how the energy travels (the waves), and how we
    size and locate the event (the scales).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="rebound, waves, magnitude vs intensity, the destructive sequence" widget="equationStep" config={seisCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four cards in order, because they
    are the lesson's chain of cause and effect — strain stores energy on a locked fault,
    the rupture converts it to waves, the waves are read as both a size (magnitude) and a
    map of shaking (intensity), and the damage follows a predictable sequence. Keep the
    chain in mind; each section below unpacks one card.
  </p>

  <h2>Stress, strain and faulting: how the energy builds</h2>
  <p>
    Tectonic forces put rock under <strong>stress</strong> (force per area), and rock responds
    with <strong>strain</strong> (deformation). Under low stress rock deforms elastically —
    bending like a spring, recovering fully when the stress releases. Push further and it
    behaves plastically, flowing without breaking (deep, hot rock does this). Push past its
    strength near the surface and it fractures. A <strong>fault</strong> is such a fracture
    along which the two sides have moved, and the three fault families record the three stress
    directions: <strong>normal faults</strong> from extension (the Basin and Range's stretched
    landscape), <strong>reverse (thrust) faults</strong> from compression (the Himalaya, and
    every subduction megathrust — the source of history's largest quakes), and
    <strong>strike-slip faults</strong> from shear (the San Andreas, sides grinding past each
    other). Folds are the same story in rock that deforms without breaking — bent strata are
    frozen strain.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="pair each fault family with the stress it records" widget="match" config={seisFaultMatch} />
  <p>
    The earthquake mechanism is <strong>elastic rebound</strong> (H. F. Reid, after the 1906
    San Francisco quake): plates keep moving, but the fault surface is locked by friction, so
    the rock on either side bends and strains for decades or centuries — storing elastic
    energy like a drawn bow. When the stress finally exceeds the friction, the fault slips in
    seconds: the strained rock snaps back, the stored energy radiates as seismic waves, and
    the two sides of the fault are offset by metres. The rupture patch can be enormous — the
    2004 Sumatra–Andaman quake unzipped roughly 1,300 km of megathrust. The <em>hypocentre</em>
    (focus) is the point where rupture began; the <em>epicentre</em> is its surface projection
    — the label you see on maps.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="call the length of the rupture" widget="estimate" config={seisSumatraEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "earthquakes happen because of a build-up of pressure below
    the crust." The energy is not pressure in a chamber — it is <em>elastic strain</em> in cold
    rock that is being bent by moving plates and held back by friction. Every major quake
    relieves strain that took decades to centuries to accumulate, on faults that are always
    moving in the long run. And a quake's "trigger" question (did the Moon, the weather?)
    dissolves here: the strain was always going to release; only its exact second is
    unknowable.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> rock stores plate motion as elastic strain for decades or
    centuries until the locked fault slips in seconds — and the three fault families
    record the three stress directions that do it. Next: the instrument that turns the
    rupture into a record.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the signal: the seismometer</h2>
  <p>
    The instrument that makes the whole science possible is conceptually beautiful: a
    <strong>seismometer</strong> is a suspended mass that stays still while the ground moves
    under it, so the relative motion between mass and frame is the ground's motion, recorded
    as a seismogram. Modern instruments sense that motion with electromagnetic pickups and
    resolve ground displacements far smaller than an atom — broadband seismometers record
    everything from passing trucks to the Earth's tides and the planet's free oscillations
    after great quakes. Networks matter more than instruments: the global arrays (and
    CTBTO's monitoring stations, built to police nuclear tests) place thousands of
    stations worldwide, so any quake above magnitude 4 is located within minutes, anywhere.
    The same networks detect explosions, which is how seismic monitoring became the
    treaty-verification tool of the nuclear age — plate tectonics' hazard doubled as
    geopolitics' referee.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — how a seismometer works" widget="judge" config={seisSeismoJudge} />

  <h2>Seismic waves: the message and the map</h2>
  <p>
    Rupture energy travels as waves, and their speed ranking is the seismologist's clock.
    <strong>P-waves</strong> (primary, compressional — push–pull, like sound) are fastest at
    6–8 km/s in crust, travel through solids <em>and</em> liquids, and arrive first, usually
    as a sharp jolt. <strong>S-waves</strong> (secondary, shear — side-to-side) run about half
    as fast, travel only through solids, and shake harder. <strong>Surface waves</strong>
    (Love and Rayleigh) travel slowest along the surface but carry the largest amplitudes —
    they are what sways high-rises and does most structural damage. The P-minus-S time lag
    grows with distance, so one station says "somewhere on a circle"; three stations triangulate
    the <strong>epicentre</strong> — the classic classroom exercise that is also how it is
    actually done at scale. And the waves' deeper value is internal: S-waves fail to cross the
    outer core (liquid), P-wave shadow zones map its size, and every layer of the Earth
    (the interior lesson) was discovered by reading quake echoes — seismology is the only
    instrument that has ever imaged the inside of a planet. Waves also carry rank: a
    magnitude is measurable from the wave amplitude once the distance is known, which is why
    any network can size a quake before any human has felt it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="fill the blanks — the wave ranking" widget="cloze" config={seisWaveCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a seismometer records the ground's motion, and the wave
    arrival order — P first, S second, surface last — acts as a clock whose P-minus-S lag
    locates the quake and whose amplitudes size it. Next: what those sizes actually mean.
  </p>

  <h2>Magnitude and intensity: two different questions</h2>
  <p>
    How big was it? and how bad did it feel? are different questions with different scales.
    <strong>Magnitude</strong> measures the energy released — one number per earthquake, on a
    logarithmic scale where each whole step is about 32 times more energy. The modern measure
    is <strong>moment magnitude (Mw)</strong>, computed from the fault area that ruptured and
    the slip across it; the old Richter scale (1935) was a California-specific proxy that
    saturates for great quakes and is now historical. Anchor the numbers: M6 ≈ a moderate
    city-damaging quake; M7 (Haiti 2010) ≈ several thousand Hiroshima bombs of seismic
    energy; the 32×-per-step rule makes an M9 tens of thousands of times an M6 — and the
    2004 Sumatra quake (M9.1) released most of that in a few minutes of rupture.
    <strong>Intensity</strong> (Modified Mercalli, I–XII) measures shaking experienced at a
    place — and it varies with distance, local geology and building quality. Soft sediments
    amplify shaking (Mexico City 1985: damage 350 km from the epicentre, because the city is
    built on an old lakebed that rang like a bowl). So the same earthquake generates one
    magnitude and a whole map of intensities — the basis of ShakeMaps, which emergency
    responders use before any survey team arrives.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="drop the marker — the 2004 quake's magnitude" widget="place" config={seisMagPlace} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the magnitude scale runs from 1 to 10, and a 7 is a
  bit worse than a 6." There is no upper limit, and the scale is logarithmic: each whole
  step releases about 32 times more energy, so an M9 is not one point worse than an M6
  but tens of thousands of times worse. And the old Richter scale was a California-specific
  proxy that saturates for great quakes — the modern measure is moment magnitude.</p>

  <h2>What actually kills: the destructive sequence</h2>
  <p>
    Ground shaking itself kills few people directly — <strong>buildings falling</strong> kill
    most. This is why the death toll is a property of the built environment as much as the
    geology: the 2010 comparison stands as the lesson's hardest data point — Haiti (M7.0,
    no enforced building codes) killed perhaps 160,000–300,000; Chile (M8.8, six weeks later,
    500 times the energy, strict codes) killed a few hundred. Codes, not luck. The secondary
    hazards have their own physics: <strong>liquefaction</strong> turns water-saturated sand
    into a fluid when shaking breaks its grain structure (buildings sink; Niigata 1964's
    famous photo shows apartment blocks lying on their sides intact); <strong>landslides</strong>
    bury valleys; <strong>fires</strong> from broken gas mains outdo the shaking (San Francisco
    1906 burned for days; Kobe 1995 burned 1990s Japan); and <strong>tsunamis</strong> carry
    the ocean: a subsea megathrust heaves the seafloor, the water column above follows, and
    the wave — barely noticeable at sea, metres high onshore — crosses the ocean at jet speed.
    The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami killed about 228,000 around the basin; since then, Indian
    Ocean warning buoys and the global system have caught successors (2011 Japan's tsunami
    warning was issued within minutes — though the waves there were beyond any evacuation's
    reach). The tsunami physics is worth one sentence: in deep water the wave travels at
    √(g × depth) — about 700–800 km/h across the Pacific — with a wavelength of hundreds of
    kilometres but a height under a metre; only when the floor shoals does the wave slow,
    compress and rear up. That is why ships at sea pass through tsunamis unharmed, and coast
    dwellers must not.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="rebuild a tsunami — from seafloor to shore" widget="order" config={seisTsunamiOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the danger of an earthquake is set by its magnitude."
  The death toll is a property of the built environment as much as the geology: ground
  shaking kills few people directly — buildings falling kill most. Haiti 2010 (M7.0, no
  enforced codes) killed perhaps 160,000–300,000; Chile 2010 (M8.8, 500 times the energy,
  strict codes) killed a few hundred. Codes, not luck, decide which magnitude becomes a
  catastrophe.</p>

  <h2>Forecasting versus prediction: the honest line</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Prediction</strong> — "a quake will strike here on this date" — remains unsolved,
    and most seismologists doubt it ever will be: fault systems are chaotic, and the final
    trigger of a rupture happens at scales we cannot yet observe. <strong>Forecasting</strong> —
    probabilities over years to decades — is mature and useful: "a 30% chance of M ≥ 6.7 in
    the San Francisco Bay region by 2043" drives land-use rules and building codes, which are
    the true life-savers. Two records sharpen the picture. <strong>Paleoseismology</strong>
    extends the human record back thousands of years by trenching faults and reading
    prehistoric ruptures in displaced strata and drowned forests: the Cascadia megathrust
    (Pacific Northwest) breaks roughly every 500 years on average, last in January 1700 — a
    date pinned not by geology alone but by Japanese records of an orphan tsunami with no
    parent quake. And <strong>induced seismicity</strong> is real and human: wastewater
    injected deep into wells (more than fracking itself) raises pore pressure on stressed
    faults — Oklahoma's quake rate rose roughly 300-fold in the 2010s and fell when injection
    was curtailed. Human-caused, therefore human-manageable. Where the risk concentrates is
    likewise mappable: most large quakes occur on <strong>plate boundaries</strong> (the Ring
    of Fire again), but intraplate quakes happen too — the 1811–12 New Madrid quakes struck
    the stable interior of North America and rang church bells in Boston — meaning hazard
    maps must rest on geology, not just borders.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each practice under the honesty of its claim" widget="sort-buckets" config={seisForecastSort} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Which seismic waves arrive first, and which do most structural damage?</li>
    <li>One earthquake, two towns: one ruined, one merely rattled. How many magnitudes and how many intensities does that event have?</li>
    <li>Chile 2010 released 500 times the energy of Haiti 2010 six weeks later, yet killed a few hundred rather than hundreds of thousands. Explain the difference.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) P-waves arrive first (fastest, push–pull, harmless
    enough that early-warning systems exploit the head start); surface waves travel
    slowest but carry the largest amplitudes, so they sway high-rises and do most
    structural damage. (2) One magnitude — energy released, a single number per quake —
    and a whole map of intensities, which vary with distance, local geology and building
    quality. (3) The energy difference is real, but shaking kills few directly: Haiti's
    toll was a property of unenforced building codes, Chile's survival a property of
    strict ones. Codes, not luck.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An earthquake is elastic rebound: strain stored for decades, released in seconds.</li>
    <li>Normal, reverse and strike-slip faults record extension, compression and shear.</li>
    <li>P-waves are fastest and pass through liquid; S-waves cannot, which is how the outer core was found.</li>
    <li>Magnitude is one logarithmic number per quake (about 32 times more energy per step); intensity is a map of shaking.</li>
    <li>Soft sediments amplify shaking — Mexico City 1985 rang like a bowl 350 km from the epicentre.</li>
    <li>Buildings, not shaking, kill most people; building codes are the true life-saver.</li>
    <li>Forecasting gives probabilities over years; date-and-place prediction remains unsolved.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate <em>Volcanoes &amp; Igneous Processes</em> lesson continues along the
    same plate boundaries, where melting rock releases stored energy of its own, and
    <em>Oceans &amp; Currents</em> takes the tsunami's other half — what the heaved
    seafloor does to the water column above it. Together the three lessons complete the
    plate-tectonics picture the foundational interior lesson opened.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Early warning is the live frontier</strong>, and it works on a simple inequality:
    radio is faster than rock. P-waves are harmless but fast; S- and surface waves are
    damaging but slower; an electronic alert outpaces both. Japan's system stops Shinkansen
    trains and cuts factory lines automatically; Mexico City's sirens buy up to 60 seconds
    (the city's lakebed geology delays the worst shaking further); ShakeAlert now serves the
    US West Coast, issuing phone alerts seconds before strong shaking. Seconds stop trains,
    surgeries and elevators — and, multiplied over millions of people, they are worth as many
    lives as any code. The next stage is density: fibre-optic cables repurposed as strain
    sensors (distributed acoustic sensing) can turn the internet's own infrastructure into a
    planetary seismometer.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="the rock's head start versus the alert — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={seisWarnCompare} />
  <p>
    Quantitatively, the field's centre of gravity is the <strong>seismic moment</strong>:
    rigidity × rupture area × slip — the physical quantity moment magnitude reports, and the
    reason great quakes are so disproportionate (rupture area grows quadratically with
    length, so a quake twice as long releases far more than twice the energy). Earthquake
    statistics obey the <strong>Gutenberg–Richter law</strong>: for every whole-number step
    down in magnitude, quakes are about ten times more frequent — which is why magnitude-3
    events are nuisance news and magnitude-9 events are generational. And the deep structure
    keeps teaching: aftershock sequences follow their own decay law (Omori's, 1894), "slow
    slip" events on megathrusts release strain silently over weeks (discovered only in the
    2000s, now monitored by GPS), and the largest earthquakes ever recorded — Chile 1960,
    M9.5 — rang the whole planet like a bell, with free oscillations measurable for days. The
    Earth rings; we have only recently learned to listen.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="find the backwards claim — the scaling law" widget="spot-error" config={seisGRSpot} />
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Reid, elastic rebound (1910); USGS Earthquake Hazards Program; the 2004 and 2011 tsunami reports; ShakeAlert documentation; OpenGeology, An Introduction to Geology, ch. 9. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
